    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "3d73fe66794c99c469568c628f7d18d8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1807,
    "chunk_text": "Accordingly, a provision of Rs. 57.2 billion has been allocated in year 2025 for the above programmes for school students, and necessary measures are to be taken in the future to increase the number of students receiving those benefits.\n\n3. The GCE (O/L) Examination for the year 2025 is scheduled to be held from 17th to 26th February. Those days fall during the holy month of Ramadan for Muslims, and in previous years also - for example, in 2024 - exams had been held during the Ramadan period.\n\nA discussion was held regarding this matter with the Provincial Director of Education, Eastern Province and other Directors and it was decided that it is possible to conduct this examination without any issue.\n\nAt another discussion held with the General Secretary of the All Ceylon Jamiyyathul Ulama, ACJU, Mr. Arkam Nooramith, the former Commissioner General of Examinations, Mr. H.J.M.C. Amith Jayasundara and the Commissioner of Examinations (School Examinations Organization and Results), Mrs. M. Jeevarani Punitha, it was stated that conducting the examination during this period would not be problematic.\n\nThe Examination Calendar had been designed considering the factors raised in part 3 of the Question. Accordingly, the Grade 5 Scholarship Examination and the GCE (A/L) Examination falls in early August and the GCE (O/L) Exam falls in December every year. That is the schedule we are trying to return to.\n\n4. The third academic semester for students currently studying in Grade 11 was scheduled to end on 19th December, 2025. That happened and accordingly, those students had almost two months to wait for\n\nthe examination. During that period they were on study leave and had ample time to study. If we further postpone this exam, students will have to wait for a longer time to sit for the exam."
  },
  {
