    "chunk_text": "Sir, Sri Lanka\u2019s geographical location places us strategically at the crossroads of major maritime routes. This has blessed us with trade and business opportunity. But, it has also made us vulnerable. International drug syndicates exploit the vastness of our high seas. They operate beyond conventional territorial boundaries; they move across jurisdictions with precision and impunity and when intercepted, gaps in domestic law create procedural and prosecutorial challenges.\n\nSir, under our current legal framework, within our national laws, trafficking of dangerous drugs in the high\n\n1103 1104\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0d85\u0da2\u0dd2\u0dad\u0dca \u0db4\u0dd3. \u0db4\u0dd9\u0dbb\u0dda\u0dbb\u0dcf \u0dbb\u0dcf \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dcf] \u0dcf]\n\n2026 \u0db4\u0dd9\u0db6\u0dbb\u0dc0\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dd2 19\u0db6\u0dbb\u0dc0\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dd2 19\n\nThis Amendment to the Judicature Act empowers our High Court with forum jurisdiction to hear and determine such offences. This is responsible governance; this is legislative maturity and this is closing the door before the tide overwhelms us. But, while we debate about the high seas, let us remember what is at stake on our own shores. Every shipment intercepted offshore represents thousands of doses of narcotics that would otherwise infiltrate our streets. Every successful prosecution should send a strong signal to international criminal networks that Sri Lanka is not a soft target and that our Island is not their hunting ground. So, every strengthened law is a shield and armour around our youth."
